## "Get Woke, Go Broke" (April 17, 2023)

Carlson opened this essay with his customary disclaimer that nothing he writes about the economy, the stock market or investing constitutes financial advice, and with George Carlin's line, "it's a big club, and you ain't in it."{n} He set out two readings of the quote — random misfortune versus a governing cabal — and stated plainly that he sides with the second: narratives are pushed, agendas are implemented, and politicians, celebrities and CEOs are selected from a club most people can never join.{n}

In an unusually personal passage, Carlson described himself as a meathead who lifts weights twice daily, had just finished months of German Volume Training, eats a largely animal-based diet, is happily married with children, and attends a Bible-believing church weekly; he added that he recognized Covid as a deep state narrative three months before the lockdowns.{n} His argument from this self-portrait was that the cabal despises trans people more than it despises conservative Christians like him: where he has merely been cancelled and ignored, he said, mentally unwell people are being actively promoted so that they destroy their own lives as pawns in the cabal's plan.{n}

Carlson recounted telling his 13- and 15-year-old sons about a TikTok trans influencer sponsored by [[bud-light|Bud Light]] and Nike, only for them to reply that nobody they knew had any idea who that was.{n} He drew from this the conclusion that mentally well children in religiously conservative homes are largely insulated from these narratives, that the real problem lies with indoctrinated parents, and that the correct response is to "worry less, pray more."{n} He also stated that pushing anything sexual on children is child abuse that should be punished to the fullest extent of the law, and that the Dalai Lama should already have been stripped of his position.{n}

Turning to the title subject, Carlson argued that corporations upsetting the majority of their customer base to appease a tiny non-customer fraction defies all logic, citing corporate billions sent to [[black-lives-matter|BLM]] during the 2020 riots, [[coca-cola|Coca-Cola]]'s "less white" employee training, and advertising that abruptly erased white men or cast them as objects of ridicule.{n} He explained the behavior through [[esg|ESG]]: a retail-investor screening product repurposed into a compliance regime enforced by Soros-funded bodies such as the Human Rights Campaign and backed by exclusion from [[blackrock|BlackRock]], [[vanguard-group|Vanguard]] and [[state-street|State Street]] funds.{n} Hence his formulation — get woke and lose customers, refuse and be expelled from the club; either way the company loses value — and his call for consumers to boycott woke companies so they cannot be both prosperous and woke.{n} He closed on the unresolved question of whether [[larry-fink|Larry Fink]] is a reformed club member or a self-destroying one, noting that his own uncertainty is itself evidence that fifth-generation warfare is in play, and that in the end God wins.{n}

